 11_4	No. Suppose some steady flow X did end here, so that A is e to the X. Then X and A commute: doing one and then the other gives the same result in either order. Here is why. A is a sum of powers of X: the identity, plus X, plus a half of X squared, and so on. Multiplying X by any power of X, on either side, gives the next power, so X slides past every term of the sum.
 11_5	Now see what commuting costs. Take a vector with two nonzero parts, and send it along both routes: first A and then X, or first X and then A. If X has an entry off its diagonal, the two routes end in different places. Each part of the gap is an off-diagonal entry of X, times the difference between A's two stretches, times one part of the vector. Those stretches differ, so the routes can only agree when every entry off the diagonal is zero.
 11_6	So X is diagonal. Its flow only stretches the two axes, each by e raised to a real rate times the time. A real exponential is always positive, so this flow never reverses either axis, but A reverses both. A half turn and a stretch each have a steady flow of their own, and running one after the other gets here. But no single steady flow does both.
