 11_8	Allow complex matrices, and the obstacle disappears. A flip is a half turn in a complex direction, with logarithm pi times i. Every invertible complex matrix has a complex logarithm. Only the singular ones, which squash the plane flat, have none, for the same reason zero had none.
 12_1	Look back along the way. On the positive numbers the exponential forgets nothing. Every output has exactly one input, and the logarithm is a perfect ruler for ratios, the keyboard and the slide rule.
 12_2	Fold the ruler into a dial, and it forgets the decade. In the complex plane the exponential does that folding by itself, and forgets the laps. A branch cut is a decision about laps, and the product rule keeps a lap count.
 12_3	With matrices the exponential forgets even more: whole families of flows end in the same place. And some places cannot be reached by any single steady real flow at all. In every setting, a logarithm asks the same question. Which steady motion got us here?
 12_4	One more motion, now in three dimensions. Here is a solid cube, and a marked axis: a rod through the centres of its top and bottom faces. A steady spin about that rod carries every corner round its own circle about the rod, all at the same rate, while the rod itself stays put. Follow one corner: spinning at half a lap per unit of time, after one unit it has gone exactly halfway round its circle. Every other corner has too, so the whole cube has made a half turn about the rod.
 12_5	Here is the puzzle. How many different steady spins end at exactly this half turn after one unit of time? Think about the laps, the direction of spin, and the axis.
-12_6	Here is the answer, one hint at a time. Laps first: one and a half laps per unit of time also ends here, and so does two and a half, and so does any odd number of half laps. Direction next: half a lap the other way lands every corner in the same place. Last, the axis: a finished spin leaves every point of its own axis where it was, and our half turn leaves only the rod's points in place, so any other axis is ruled out. So infinitely many steady spins end here, all about this one rod. The two slowest, half a lap each way, turn by exactly pi, the very edge of the principal window, and neither is slower, so this half turn has no principal logarithm. For the number minus one, a convention broke the same tie; here the honest answer is the whole family.
